4. Locking Mechanism Catalogue
Terminological Verification Note: Standard Japanese terms verified against the OMR linguistic corpus and Shuzo Fujimoto’s polyhedral texts include: tomeshiro (止め代 — locking tab/allowance), kabuse-ori (かぶせ折り — outside reverse fold), nakawari-ori (中割り折り — inside reverse fold), shizume-ori (沈め折り — sink fold), dan-ori (段折り — pleat/crimp step), and kado (角 — physical paper corner) vs. kaku (角 — geometric angle).

4.1 Tuck Fold & Pocket-and-Flap Tuck Lock (Sashikomi / Tomeshiro Tab Lock)
Construction: A protruding triangular or trapezoidal flap (tomeshiro / 止め代) is creased along a hinge line and inserted into a pre-existing multi-layer pocket formed by folded walls.

Interacting Parts: Male insertion flap (tab), female pocket opening (two or more stacked layers bounded by closed crease edges), and the hinge crease connecting the tab to the main body.

Geometric Relationship for Retention: The tab’s insertion vector is roughly perpendicular to the primary opening/unfolding vector of the host walls. Once inside, the pocket walls constrain the tab against out-of-plane rotation, while the pocket's closed crease boundary blocks lateral translation.

Purely Geometric vs. Physical: Hybrid (Geometric + Physical). Geometrically, the pocket blocks out-of-plane separation, but along the 1D withdrawal axis (pulling the tab straight back out), retention depends entirely on sheet bending stiffness (curving the tab to enter the slot, then elastic recovery pressing it against the pocket walls) and Coulomb friction between paper surfaces.

Reversibility: Fully reversible.

Non-Destructive Release: Yes, by elastically bowing the tab or widening the pocket mouth to withdraw the tab along its insertion axis.

Sequence Dependency: Strict. The pocket walls must be formed and positioned before the tab is flexed and inserted into the pocket slot.

Formal vs. Procedural Description: Currently described procedurally in origami literature (e.g., Fujimoto’s uchigawa yori fusagu); lacks a standard closed-form rigid-origami equation because insertion requires temporary non-rigid elastic bending of the flap.

Confidence Level: High.

Source or Evidence: Fujimoto (Twist Origami / polyhedral construction notes, p. 36 & p. 109).

