4.5 Closed Sink Lock (Nakawari-shizume-ori / Enclosed Inversion Lock)
Construction: A pyramidal corner or polygonal ridge is pushed inside a closed pocket along a closed loop of creases (shizume-ori / 沈め折り) without opening the surrounding layers (closed sink), inverting all interior creases.

Interacting Parts: Inverted interior cone/prism, surrounding outer wall facets, and the closed perimeter crease ring.

Geometric Relationship for Retention: The perimeter crease ring forms a constricted aperture; once the pyramidal vertex is inverted inside, unfolding the outer walls requires simultaneously un-inverting the trapped inner cone, which collides with the outer walls.

Purely Geometric vs. Physical: Purely Geometric / Kinematic Barrier. Even in a rigid, frictionless model, there is no continuous rigid folding path from the closed-sunk state back to the open state without self-intersection or elastic buckling of the facets (a topological/kinematic trap).

Reversibility: Technically reversible, but requires high elastic deformation ("popping" the paper inside-out).

Non-Destructive Release: Difficult; often causes creasing/crumpling of the facets during forced reversal because no rigid homotopy exists.

Sequence Dependency: Strict. A closed sink cannot be folded rigidly from the flat state; it requires sequential non-rigid buckling during assembly.

Formal vs. Procedural Description: Recognized in computational origami as a classic example of a rigidly reachable folded state with no rigid folding motion from 
