6.2 Traditional Origami Boat (Fune / Sampan — Inversion & Hull Locking)
Shape-Creating & Auxiliary Folds:

Initial grid/blintz folds and diagonal pre-creases (ROLE_AUX) establish corner flaps.

Side-wall folds create the triangular bow, stern, and gunwales (ROLE_FORM).

Kinematic & Structural Transformation:

Turning the model inside-out (a global inversion maneuver related to reverse/sink kinematics) transforms flat folded pockets into a hollow 3D hull (ROLE_FORM + ROLE_STRUCT).

Retention Mechanism:

The bow and stern peaks are held upright by coupled vertex incompatibility and layer-wrap friction at the gunwale roll-folds (ROLE_LOCK). If the gunwale folds are unrolled, the hull collapses flat; thus, the gunwale creases simultaneously serve ROLE_FORM (rim edge), ROLE_STRUCT (hoop stiffness), and ROLE_LOCK (preventing hull splaying).

6.3 Candy-Wrapper / Packet-End Closure (Twisted or Pleated-and-Tucked Closure)
Connection vs. Lock Separation:

Shape-Creating (ROLE_FORM): Longitudinal tube/pouch folds form the cylindrical or rectangular container body.

Connecting (ROLE_CONNECT): Flattening the open end and folding it over once or twice brings opposing walls into overlapping contact, closing the aperture topologically—but elastic memory immediately springs the flap open if released.

Locking (ROLE_LOCK): Either (a) folding the corners inward and tucking the distal lip into the transverse pleat pocket (pocket-and-flap lock, Section 4.1), or (b) crimping the pleated end with a high-curvature Z-fold (crimp lock, Section 4.4) or twist-pinch. Here, the connection fold closes the opening, while the subsequent tuck/crimp locks the closure.

